\subsubsection{The seven-witness parity predicate and its finite correction}
\label{subsec:original-seven-parity}
Put $B=17b$. The predicate used here is exactly the existence of
$t,U,e,p,q,s,h\in\mathbb Q$ satisfying
\begin{align}
 U(1+bt^2)&=b(1-bt^2),\label{eq:seven-param}\\
 p^2&=B(1-e)(1+3e),\label{eq:seven-first}\\
 h&=e(B-U^2),\label{eq:seven-h}\\
 Bq^2&=(s^2-Bh)(s^2+3Bh).\label{eq:seven-second}
\end{align}
Denote it by $\Psi(b)$. No nonzero conditions are added to this formula.

\begin{lemma}[The exact one-sided assertion]
\label{lem:original-seven-parity-soundness}
If $b\ne0$ and $\Psi(b)$, then $v_\ell(b)$ is not a negative odd
integer for any prime $\ell\notin\{2,3,17\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $k=v_\ell(b)<0$ is odd. Equation~\eqref{eq:seven-param}
forces $1+bt^2\ne0$: if it vanished, its right side would be $2b\ne0$.
If $t=0$, then $U=b$. Otherwise $v_\ell(bt^2)$ is odd and nonzero,
so $(1-bt^2)/(1+bt^2)$ is a unit. Thus $v_\ell(U)=k$ in all cases,
and $v_\ell(B-U^2)=2k$.

